<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">15 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 15 May, from 392 to 1957.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/392">392 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,634 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Valentinian II is found hanged at Vienne</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The young western emperor was found dead in his residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Whether he was murdered by his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a> or took his own life is still debated; civil war followed.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">589 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,437 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">King Authari of the Lombards marries Theodelinda at Verona</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, daughter of the Bavarian duke Garibald I, at Verona, in a political alliance meant to reinforce his royal position. A Catholic, she later encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a> to move from Arian to Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">908 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,118 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Constantine VII is crowned Byzantine co-emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus</a>, son of Emperor Leo VI and his fourth wife, Zoe Karbonopsina, was crowned Byzantine co-emperor. Nominally emperor from 913 and in real power from 945, he sponsored the treatise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1252">1252 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">774 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Innocent IV authorises torture by inquisitors</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The bull Ad extirpanda permitted secular authorities to use torture to extract confessions from suspected heretics. It set a precedent for inquisitorial practice for centuries.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1602">1602 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">424 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bartholomew Gosnold names Cape Cod</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">English navigator Bartholomew Gosnold reached the Massachusetts peninsula and named it for the abundant cod his crew caught there. His voyage was an early English exploration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1618">1618 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">408 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Johannes Kepler confirms his third law of planetary motion</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Kepler found that the square of a planet's orbital period is proportional to the cube of its distance from the Sun. Published in Harmonices Mundi, the law later underpinned Newton's theory of gravitation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1940">1940 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">86 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nylon stockings go on sale across the United States</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Shoppers bought some four million pairs of DuPont <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a> stockings on the first day of nationwide sales. The synthetic fibre transformed fashion and was soon diverted to wartime parachutes.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1957">1957 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">69 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Britain tests its first thermonuclear weapon design</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Grapple saw a British bomb dropped near Malden Island in the Pacific. Its yield fell short of a true hydrogen bomb, but later tests that year succeeded and secured US nuclear cooperation.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 15 May <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Sejong the Great</span><span class="tp">1397–1450</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/claudio-monteverdi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/claudio-monteverdi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Claudio Monteverdi</span><span class="tp">1567–1643</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/klemens-von-metternich.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Klemens von Metternich</span><span class="tp">1773–1859</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/juan-nepomuceno-almonte"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juan-nepomuceno-almonte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Juan Nepomuceno Almonte</span><span class="tp">1803–1869</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/max-frisch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/max-frisch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Max Frisch</span><span class="tp">1911–1991</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 15 May <span class="n">7</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/valentinian-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Valentinian II</span><span class="tp">371–392</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/li-cunxu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Li Cunxu</span><span class="tp">885–926</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yuri-dolgoruky.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Yuri Dolgoruky</span><span class="tp">c. 1099–1157</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/braulio-carrillo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/braulio-carrillo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Braulio Carrillo</span><span class="tp">1800–1845</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vladko-macek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Vladko Maček</span><span class="tp">1879–1964</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-menzies"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/robert-menzies.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Robert Menzies</span><span class="tp">1894–1978</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/carlos-fuentes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/carlos-fuentes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Carlos Fuentes</span><span class="tp">1928–2012</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
